Answer: The Animal class is created first. It contains the ownerComesHome() method, which displays a message when called. The method uses void because it does not return a value, and it has no parameters.

```java
class Animal {
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Animal welcomes the owner");
}
}
```

The child classes extend Animal and override this method to define their own behaviours.

Answer: The Dog class extends Animal using the extends keyword. It overrides the ownerComesHome() method to describe how the dog welcomes the owner.

```java
class Dog extends Animal {
@Override
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println(“Dog welcomes by jumping, spinning, and                revolving around the owner");
}
}
```

The @Override annotation indicates that the method is intended to override an inherited method.

Answer: Both classes extend Animal and provide their own implementations of ownerComesHome().

```java
class Cat extends Animal {
@Override
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Cat welcomes by sleeping");
}
}
class Parrot extends Animal {
@Override
```

void ownerComesHome() {

```java
System.out.println("Parrot welcomes by screaming");
}
}
```

The method name is the same in all the child classes, but the implementation differs. The cat welcomes the owner by sleeping, while the parrot welcomes the owner by screaming.

Answer: A class defines the structure and behaviour of objects. Since the ownerComesHome() methods in the example are non-static instance methods, we create objects to call them.

```java
Dog d = new Dog();
Cat c = new Cat();
Parrot p = new Parrot();
```

The new keyword creates each object, and the reference to that object is assigned to a variable. The variables d, c, and p are then used to call the respective methods.

Answer: The dot (.) operator is used to access and call an instance method through a reference variable.

```java
d.ownerComesHome();
c.ownerComesHome();
p.ownerComesHome();
```

Each statement calls the ownerComesHome() method through the corresponding reference. The implementation defined in the respective child class executes.

